Running log: why does the kernel's genic variance fall at once under the move? (exploratory, 2026-11-14)
Script: validate/genic_decline.jl. Findings: validate/genic_decline.md. Hypotheses are written
here before the test that addresses them.

== setup ==

Established (validate/spectrum_diagnosis.md 2(b), spectrum_ld_dev.log step 5): on the published
protocol (continuous move from 10000, N = 1000, QTSim's single crossover) the kernel's genic
variance is at 0.56–0.98 of its pre-move value at +100; :spectrum, with or without ld = :dynamic,
driven by each run's own lag, S and survivors, is at 0.98–1.15 and catches up near +500; its only
decline is Barton's linked-selection term.

Typical parameters of the discovery inputs: μ = 1e-4 per locus per gamete (4Nμ = 0.4 per locus,
the house-of-cards end: most loci carry 0 or 1 segregating lineage), L = 100–5000 (Lμ = 0.01–0.5
per gamete), α²/V_P ≈ 0.01 (α ≈ 0.1 phenotypic SD), rate ≈ 0.1–0.3 SD per generation.

Accounting (exact, per generation, genic units G = 2 Σ_l v_l, population denominators):
  G_A → G_π (deterministic selection on genotypes, π = E[W | g]) → G_W (+ environmental noise)
      → G_S (+ Bernoulli survival) → G_O (+ sampling of parents/gametes) → G_M = next G_A (+ mutation).
  Selection G_π − G_A = within [2Σ Cov(π/π̄, (x − x̄)²) = k3 (2βΣκ3) + stab (rest)]
                       + own2LD [−2(1+φ)²Σo²] + hitch [−2Σε²] + nonlin.
  o_l = β Var(y_l)/2 (own response), k_l = β(Cov(g, y_l) − Var(y_l))/2 = φ o_l + ε_l (linked).
  Expected noise: Esurv (Bernoulli(π) first-order formula), Erepro = −G_S/(2N).
Spectrum analogs: sel ↔ within + own2LD + nonlin; Barton ↔ hitch; −g/(2n) ↔ env + bern + repro; mutation ↔ mutation.

Hypotheses, with what each predicts for the accounting (stated before any run):
 (a) Selection-enhanced drift (Robertson 1961): heritable fitness variance in the genetic background
     lowers N_e at once. Given the state, the drift of one generation has a fixed expectation
     (env + bern + repro ≈ −G/(2n)), so Robertson's effect can appear only as the response of a
     locus to its association with the rest of the genome: predicts hitch large from t = 0 and
     much larger than Barton's term in the driven spectrum; noise terms ≈ their expectations.
 (b) Continuum of alleles vs infinite sites: within-locus allele competition. Predicts the decline in
     the within/own2LD terms (k3 negative early) at loci with several alleles, a large share of
     variance at multi-allelic loci, and that sites10 (10× loci at μ/10, the same V_m and effects)
     removes most of the early decline.
 (c) Sweeps of pre-existing favourable alleles at intermediate frequency (third cumulant): predicts
     k3 (and within) negative early, the kernel's folded variance spectrum richer at intermediate
     frequency than the spectrum's equilibrium, the spectrum's sel term less negative than the
     kernel's deterministic term.
 (d) The fall of survivors: predicts noise terms more negative than −G/(2n_expected), but ≈ their
     expectation given the realised survivors (the driven spectrum already uses realised n).
 (e) Hitchhiking / genetic draft under the single crossover: as (a) in the accounting (hitch), and
     the decline should vanish with many crossovers (variant many); fecundity selection should not
     remove it.
 (f) Anything else the accounting shows (e.g. nonlin, mutation, stabilising part changing under the move).

