Reading of step 1 (D + Dctl):
- The early decline is almost entirely the deterministic selection term "within",
  2Σ_l Cov(π/π̄, (x − x̄_l)²), and inside it the part beyond the own third moment ("stab").
  Before the move stab is small (e.g. 01192 −0.00034, 00854 −0.0005 per generation relative);
  in generations 50..99 it is 5–15× larger (01192 −0.0030). Cumulative 0..99: stab −0.02 to
  −0.71, k3 of either sign (−0.07 to +0.43), own2LD (own response squared) ≤ 0.04.
- hitch (random association of a locus with the rest of the genome, Robertson/draft) is small:
  0.000–0.08 over 0..99, 1–9% of the decline (largest 14771 0.078 of 0.851, 04943 0.049 of 0.40).
- Noise terms are at their expectations given the realised survivors (env + bern vs Esurv,
  repro vs −G_S/2N; differences of either sign, within run-to-run noise). Mutation is constant.
- Census: ~4 distinct alleles per segregating locus, 84–96% of the genic variance at loci with
  ≥ 3 alleles, the largest single allele 83–90% of its locus's variance. Variance by minor-allele
  frequency nearly flat on (0, 0.35], falling above.
=> (a) and (e) (hitchhiking, draft, Robertson) explain ≤ 10%; (d) noise is at expectation. The
   decline is deterministic selection within loci, of the kind (b)/(c)/(f), not yet resolved.
   stab cannot be the Gaussian quadratic term of Wright's formula: in the Gaussian model the
   stabilising coefficient is (1 − λ²/S)/S, weaker under a lag, but the kernel's grows.

Hypotheses for stab (before step 2):
 (f1) linear background: carriers of deviant alleles at l (either sign) have a worse rest of the
      genotype under the move, Cov(g − own, d_l²) < 0 (a third-order association: deviant alleles
      sit in particular haplotypes); predicts 2βΣ[Cov(g, d_l²) − κ3_l] ≈ stab.
 (f2) quadratic: the curvature of π in g with the locus's fourth moment (kurtosis) or with
      background terms Cov(g′², d_l²); predicts the quadratic part ≈ stab.
Step 2 measures stab = other3 (f1) + quad (f2) + rest, with π/π̄ − 1 regressed on g′ and
g′² − V jointly (coefficients β₂, q).

