Reading of step 2:
- (f1) confirmed, (f2) rejected: within ≈ own3 + other3; quad (incl. the locus's own kurtosis) is
  −0.03..+0.03, i.e. the Gaussian stabilising term stays small as the spectrum assumes.
- The decline is other3, and in it the SIGN-SYMMETRIC part (genic-relative over 0..99):
  12000 −0.29, 14771 −0.73, 05562 −0.24, 11865 −0.20, 14110 −0.15, 06004 −0.20, 07594 −0.31,
  00854 −0.35, 04943 −0.54, 09697 −0.08, 13132 −0.09, 09607 −0.08, 01192 −0.10. Where own3 is
  positive (the Barton–Turelli rise of favourable rare alleles: 00854 +0.43, 04943 +0.38,
  07594 +0.11, 01192 +0.16) the κ3-proportional part of other3 cancels 40–70% of it.
- Meaning: individuals carrying a deviant allele at a locus (of EITHER sign) have a lagging rest
  of the genotype, Cov(g − own, d_l²) < 0, so selection on the background removes within-locus
  variance without moving the locus's mean (hence hitch, the squared mean response, is small).
- The association is zero before the move and builds over 25–75 generations (time course
  above): it is created by the move, not a standing property switched on by β.

Interpretation (hypothesis H-link, before the counterfactuals and the theory check):
the decline is linked selection on haplotype backgrounds — Robertson's (1961) cumulative effect,
the process behind Barton's (1995) N_e term — but its autocorrelated part. A drift-generated LD
D between a site (allele) and its linked background persists ~1/(ρ + 1/2n) generations under
QTSim's tight linkage; while it persists, directional selection keeps moving the site's frequency
the same way, so minority alleles that happen to sit on poor backgrounds decline steadily, and
the expected loss of pq is first order in β (via the built-up association between frequency
change and background), not only the per-generation Var(Δp). In the per-generation accounting
the per-generation part is hitch (∝ 1/ρ, small), the autocorrelated part is other3 (∝ 1/ρ²,
large). Barton's term is the steady state of their sum, and the spectrum applies it at once,
damped by δ = 1/(2n) + V_G/S; the kernel's version starts at 0 at the move and builds as the
associations accumulate (∝ t² while t ≪ 1/(ρ + δ)), and with ρ ≈ 0 for most pairs it overtakes
the δ-capped steady state within tens of generations.
Predictions: (i) many crossovers remove most of other3 and the decline; (ii) fecundity selection
keeps it; (iii) at 4N it is smaller per generation (drift-generated LD ∝ 1/n) unless sweeps of
existing haplotypes dominate; (iv) sites10 (more loci, same map) keeps it (it is linkage, not the
continuum of alleles); (v) a time-dependent Barton term (derived below) moves the driven
spectrum's decline earlier.

== step 2b: checks requested by the coordinator (hypotheses from a parallel closure study) ==
H-c1: at the move the kernel's per-locus Σκ4/Σμ4 ≈ 0 and Σκ3 at noise level (so the spectrum's onset
      rise, a κ4-driven skew route, is spurious). H-c2: the early loss is ≈ 0.05–0.11 b²V_G per
      generation (b = λ/S), a linked-selection/hitchhiking term with no 1/(2n) factor.
