Reading: with the Gaussian dissolution term (δ includes V/S ≈ 0.05–0.2 per generation) the
associations reach their steady state within ~10 generations and the transient term equals
Barton's (bar ≈ trS everywhere): too weak, median log(model/kernel) +0.32 at +100. Without it
(δ = 1/(2n) only) the term is far too strong (median −0.92 at +100, collapse by +200): for the
tightly linked classes (ρ ≪ 1/(2n)) the linear model lets a fully linked association push the
site's frequency for ~2n generations, while in the kernel the background haplotype's sweep ends
much sooner. The kernel lies between the two bounds; the decay of an association under
directional selection (the turnover of selected backgrounds, which saturates the linear Δp = bD)
is the missing quantity, and neither δ choice is it. No term is proposed; the bracket is the result.

